1,0 $Cheilocystidia lageniform to sublecythiform, tapering in to a narrow neck with well-delimited capitulum, 4.0-6.µm wide; spores usually in part phaseoliform 14.$ Ph. brunnea
1,2 $ Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, utriform or lageniform, occasionally subcapitate but without well-delimited capitulum; spores not phaseoliform

2,3 $ Spores without germ pore, sometimes with slightly paler apical spot (callus)
2,4 $ Spores with distinct germ pore

3,0 $ Flocks of veil fibrillose and thin, usually large, fringing the entire margin of pileus; basidiocarps usually in summer and autumn 8.$ Ph. vestita
3,0 $ Flocks of veil submembranaceous, thick, often striate-sulcate, in one or more irregular patches adhering to pileus margin; basidiocarps usually in spring 3.$ Ph. aporos

4,0 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly utriform to clavate. Spores minutely verruculose under oil-immersion lens, in some collections seemingly smooth (but verruculose under SEM)13.$ Ph. utriformis
4,5 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly subcylindrical to lageniform, often subcapitate. Spores smooth. Insufficiently unravelled group of Ph. velata; see also notes under that species $$s"Groupe velata"

5,6 $ Spores on the average 7.0-8.3 µm long
5,7 $ Spores on the average(8.5)9.0-10.6 µm long

6,0 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly lageniform with slender, often subcapitate neck. Pileus centre usually rather pale when moist: yellow-brown, ochre-brown, orangey brown 9.$ Ph. velata
6,0 $ Cheilocystidia more variable, lageniform, utriform, clavate and subcylindrical. Pileus centre usually rather dark when moist: reddish brown to rusty brown 10.$ Ph. exannulata

7,0 $ Cheilo cystidia lagenifonn with long cylindrical neck,3.0-5.0(6.0)µm wide, often in part subcapitate 11.$ Ph. nemoralis
7,0 $ Cheilocystidia more variable, lageniform, utriform, clavate and subcylindrical 12. $ Ph. mutabilis
